WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:10.270
Can you construct an equilateral triangle
from a line segment AB?

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:12.790
Think about
how you can do it?

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:18.810
To construct the equilateral triangle, we
have to find the third vertex other

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.900
than ‘A’ and ‘B’.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.660
For that, let’s take a compass.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.360
We put the fixed point of compass on one of
the

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.060
points, say ‘B’.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.710
Now taking this line segment ‘AB’ as the
radius, we draw a circle

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:35.160
with ‘B’ as its centre.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.580
Similarly, without changing the length of
the compass,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.300
repeat the process by placing its fixed point
on point ‘A’.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.280
Now we see that we
have two circles with centres ‘A’ and

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.410
‘B’ and both these circles intersect at
these 2 points.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:56.780
So if we join points ‘A’ and ‘B’ to
this point, we see that they are

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:00.720
nothing but the radii of the circles.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:06.210
And since the radii of both the circles are
equal to each other and also equal to the

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:11.440
length of side AB, we get an
equilateral triangle here.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.180
We will get an equilateral triangle even if
we join

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.490
points A and B to this point here.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.730
Wasn’t it interesting?

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:25.580
Such knowledge of geometry was known to people
from ancient times…

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:30.290
Around 300 B.C.E, the Greek mathematician
Euclid combined all the

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:34.300
knowledge of geometry known at that time in
a set of 13 books called

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.350
Elements.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.420
One interesting thing Euclid did was that
he set out to prove that the

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:45.950
geometrical relations known at that time follows
logically from some simple

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:49.800
things we know intuitively to be true.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.980
Euclid showed that the knowledge of
geometry can be proven by just assuming some

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.700
things to be true.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:02.060
These five true statements form the base of
all modern geometry.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:06.479
He called
these true statements or facts as POSTULATES.

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:12.170
You’d be wondering what the
difference between axioms and postulates is!

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:16.650
Well in Geometry, they are
essentially interchangeable.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:21.510
The general meaning of a postulate is a true
statement.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.879
But in case of Euclid, a
postulate is a true statement or rather a

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:29.719
fact that is applicable in geometry
only.

00:02:29.719 --> 00:02:33.950
So in this lesson, let us take a look at the
five important postulates that

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.959
Euclid devised which helped him prove more
theorems.

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:42.590
Let’s have a look at what the first postulate
says:

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:47.859
It states that “A straight line may be drawn
from any one point to any other

00:02:47.859 --> 00:02:49.389
point.”

00:02:49.389 --> 00:02:54.409
To understand this, suppose we have two points
‘A’ and ‘B’ separated by a

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:56.400
distance.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.319
Now if we want to join them, we see that we
can draw only one

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:04.659
straight line that will connect the both of
them.

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:09.420
We can take help of Euclid’s
axiom that states “Given two distinct points,

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:13.299
there is a unique line passing
through them”.

00:03:13.299 --> 00:03:17.639
So a line from ‘A’ joining ‘B’, is
‘A B’.

00:03:17.639 --> 00:03:20.560
Similarly, the line joining ‘A’ from ‘B’
is

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.790
also ‘A B’.

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:26.590
**
The second postulate states that “A terminated

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:30.019
line can be produced
indefinitely”

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:34.959
By ‘terminated line’, we understand that
it is a line segment.

00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:39.269
This postulate
means that if we have line segment ‘A B’,

00:03:39.269 --> 00:03:43.340
we can extend both of its ends
endlessly.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.430
What about the next postulate?

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:46.430
**

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:51.159
The third postulate says that “A circle
can be drawn with any centre and any

00:03:51.159 --> 00:03:54.450
radius”.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:59.549
This postulate is self-explanatory, but for
our understanding let’s assume a line

00:03:59.549 --> 00:04:01.670
segment ‘A B’.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:07.010
Keep point ‘A’ fixed and move point ‘B’
around point ‘A’.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.081
We will
see that it will make a circle, and the length

00:04:10.081 --> 00:04:14.090
of the line segment ‘A B’ will be
the radius of the circle.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:18.019
Likewise, we can draw a circle from any point
and with

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:20.519
any radius.

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:23.340
**
The fourth postulate says that “All right

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.550
angles are equal to one another”.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:29.480
Look at a square and a rectangle.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:34.930
We see that all the interior angles of the
rectangle and square are right angles!

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:41.220
Therefore, an angle from a square will
be equal to an angle from a rectangle.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:46.810
So, these are the Euclid’s first four postulates
that will help us in having a

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:50.130
better understanding of geometry.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:55.130
In the next lesson, we will study about the
Euclid’s fifth postulate and move

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:57.090
ahead.

